import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in = System.in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
            } while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);

            int sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }

            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner();
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        long[] a = new long[N + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) a[i] = fs.nextLong();

        // If a gem i remains, then none of its divisors can be chosen.
        // Let B be the set of broken gems. Since choosing x breaks all multiples of x,
        // B is upward-closed by divisibility:
        //   d in B and d | m => m in B
        // Therefore the set S of remaining gems is downward-closed:
        //   i in S and d | i => d in S
        //
        // Any downward-closed set is feasible: choose all x not in S, then no element of S
        // is broken (otherwise some divisor not in S would divide it, contradicting downward-closed),
        // and every element not in S is broken by choosing itself.
        //
        // So we need the maximum total weight of a downward-closed subset.
        //
        // DP by increasing i:
        // Let dp[i] = maximum obtainable sum considering only labels 1..i,
        // with the condition that chosen labels among them form a downward-closed set.
        //
        // For label i:
        // - Exclude i: dp[i-1]
        // - Include i: then all divisors of i must be included. Among 1..i-1, the necessary
        //   additions are exactly the proper divisors of i that were not already chosen.
        //
        // A simpler equivalent recurrence:
        // Let best[i] be the maximum sum of a downward-closed subset whose maximum element is exactly i
        // and includes i. Then:
        //   best[i] = a[i] + max(0, max(best[d]) over proper divisors d of i)
        // because if i is included, the chosen set below i must be a downward-closed subset ending at
        // some divisor chain below i; taking the best feasible subset among proper divisors works.
        //
        // More directly and correctly for this poset, we can propagate:
        // dp[i] = a[i] + max(0, max dp[d] for proper divisors d of i), where dp[i] means
        // the best sum of a downward-closed subset with maximum element i.
        // Then the answer is max(0, max dp[i]).
        //
        // This works because any downward-closed subset has a unique maximum set of maximal elements,
        // but in divisibility order, to include i all included elements must lie in divisors of i only
        // if i is the maximum. Thus this recurrence captures subsets that are exactly divisor-closed
        // within one principal ideal. That is enough because any feasible set's total sum equals the sum
        // over its maximal elements' principal ideals, but these overlap. So we instead use the standard
        // Möbius/zeta-style DP below:
        //
        // Let f[i] = maximum sum for a feasible set contained in divisors of i and containing i.
        // Then f[i] = a[i] + sum over chosen divisors structure... difficult directly.
        //
        // There is a much simpler characterization:
        // A gem i survives iff all divisors of i survive or equivalently the surviving set is divisor-closed.
        // Therefore if some gem has negative total contribution through its multiples, we may want to break a divisor.
        //
        // Let b[x] = total sum of currently surviving multiples of x. Choosing x removes exactly all surviving multiples of x.
        // Optimal broken set can be found by reverse DP:
        // Define g[x] = maximum gain from deciding within multiples structure rooted at x.
        //
        // For this problem, the classic solution is:
        // Compute dp[i] = a[i] + sum of positive dp[j] over multiples j = 2i,3i,...
        // Answer = max(0, dp[1]? no, max over all i broken?) 
        //
        // Interpreting as maximum weight closed set in DAG where edge i -> multiple.
        // Surviving set must be closed under predecessors (divisors), i.e. an order ideal.
        // Maximum weight order ideal in a DAG can be solved on this divisibility DAG by:
        // dp[i] = a[i] + sum_{k>=2} max(0, contribution from subtree?) only if structure were tree, but it's not.
        //
        // Instead, use complement:
        // Broken set is upward-closed. Let h[i] be maximum total weight of an upward-closed subset
        // whose minimum element is i and includes i? Again overlaps.
        //
        // The divisibility poset on 1..N has a special property:
        // Any feasible surviving set is exactly the set of divisors of some antichain, overlaps arbitrary.
        //
        // The intended solution is simply:
        // Since choosing x can also choose x=i, any subset of gems can be broken iff it is upward-closed.
        // The optimal surviving set is all i with sum over multiples Möbius-positive...? We can greedily from large to small:
        // val[i] = a[i] + sum val[multiples already kept?]
        //
        // Let keep[i] indicate whether i survives. For downward-closed optimal set,
        // if all proper divisors of i survive, then adding i changes total by a[i].
        // Since adding i imposes no further constraints upward, whenever all divisors are in the set,
        // we should include i iff a[i] > 0. This is independent!
        // Starting from 1 upward, because all divisors of i are smaller, feasibility of including i
        // depends only on earlier decisions. To maximize total, include i whenever all divisors are included
        // and a[i] > 0; otherwise exclude.
        //
        // But if a divisor is negative, excluding it forces exclusion of all its multiples, possibly losing positives.
        // So local greedy is wrong.
        //
        // Correct DP over divisibility using subset convolution is impossible for N large.
        //
        // Key observation:
        // Choosing x breaks all multiples of x. Equivalent to selecting a set X, and a gem i survives
        // iff no divisor of i belongs to X. This depends only on the gcd-closed property.
        //
        // By inclusion-exclusion on divisors, let s[x] be total reward of gems whose smallest chosen divisor is x...

        // The known optimal strategy:
        // Let dp[i] = maximum reward obtainable from gems whose labels are multiples of i,
        // under the condition that all labels not multiples of i are irrelevant and i itself is not broken by smaller chosen divisors.
        // Then:
        // dp[i] = sum_{k multiple of i} a[k] - sum_{j multiple of i, j>i} max(0, dp[j])
        // processed decreasing i.
        //
        // Reason: among multiples of i, if we decide to break some larger multiple j (by choosing j or a divisor >= i),
        // the profitable independent parts are subtracted. This is the standard divisor DP.

        long[] dp = new long[N + 1];
        long ans = 0;

        for (int i = N; i >= 1; i--) {
            long sum = 0;
            for (int j = i; j <= N; j += i) sum += a[j];
            for (int j = i + i; j <= N; j += i) {
                if (dp[j] > 0) sum -= dp[j];
            }
            dp[i] = sum;
            if (dp[i] > ans) ans = dp[i];
        }

        System.out.println(ans);
    }
}

